ss="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyamazana-dlamini"><span class="enc-name">Nyamazana Dlamini</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyamazana Dlamini was a Swati queen and military leader who led one of the groups that crossed the Zimbabwean plateau during the Mfecane.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyambo-kapararidze"><span class="enc-name">Nyambo Kapararidze</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyambo Kapararidze was a ruler of the Mutapa kingdom in present-day Zimbabwe who came to the throne around 1623 and resisted growing Portuguese influence.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyamutukura-kyebambe-iii"><span class="enc-name">Nyamutukura Kyebambe III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyamutukura Kyebambe III was omukama of Bunyoro in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyaneka"><span class="enc-name">Nyaneka</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nyaneka are a Bantu-speaking people of south-western Angola, living mainly in Huíla province.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyangire-revolt"><span class="enc-name">Nyangire revolt</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1907</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nyangire revolt was a protest movement in Bunyoro in 1907 against the Ganda chiefs that the British had appointed over Nyoro districts.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyangwe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nyangwe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nyangwe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyangwe is a town on the Lualaba River in Maniema in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyanja-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nyanja-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nyanja people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nyanja are a Bantu-speaking people living around Lake Malawi and the Shire valley in Malawi, Mozambique and Zambia, closely related to the Chewa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyantio"><span class="enc-name">Nyantio</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The nyantio were the warrior aristocracy of the Kaabu Empire in the Senegambian region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nyanza-rwanda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nyanza, Rwanda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyanza is a town in the Southern Province of Rwanda that became the permanent royal capital under Yuhi V Musinga around 1899.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyasaland-african-congress"><span class="enc-name">Nyasaland African Congress</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nyasaland African Congress was the first nationwide African political organisation in the British protectorate of Nyasaland, founded in 1944.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyatapola-temple"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nyatapola-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nyatapola Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyatapola is a five-storeyed pagoda temple in Taumadhi Square, Bhaktapur, in the Kathmandu Valley of Nepal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota"><span class="enc-name">Nyatsimba Mutota</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyatsimba Mutota was the founder of the Mutapa Empire in the fifteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nyau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nyau</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyau is a secret society of the Chewa people of Malawi, Zambia and Mozambique, whose initiated men perform masked dances at funerals, initiations and memorial rites.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyaungyan-min"><span class="enc-name">Nyaungyan Min</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyaungyan Min was a son of Bayinnaung who rebuilt royal power in Upper Burma after the First Toungoo Empire collapsed in 1599.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyaya"><span class="enc-name">Nyaya</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyaya is one of the six orthodox schools of Hindu philosophy, concerned above all with logic, the means of valid knowledge and the theory of argument.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyenskans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nyenskans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nyenskans</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyenskans was a Swedish fortress built in 1611 at the confluence of the Okhta and Neva rivers, on the site of present-day Saint Petersburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Nyiginya dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nyiginya dynasty was the royal line of the Kingdom of Rwanda, whose kings bore the title mwami.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyikang"><span class="enc-name">Nyikang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyikang is the legendary founder and culture hero of the Shilluk people of South Sudan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nymphaeum-crimea"><span class="enc-name">Nymphaeum (Crimea)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nymphaeum was an ancient Greek city on the Crimean shore of the Kerch Strait, south of Panticapaeum, founded in the sixth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nymphaion"><span class="enc-name">Nymphaion</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nymphaion was a town in western Asia Minor, east of Smyrna, now Kemalpaşa near İzmir in Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nymphenburg-palace"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nymphenburg-palace.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nymphenburg Palace</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Nymphenburg Palace is a Baroque palace in western Munich, begun in 1664 as a summer residence for Elector Ferdinand Maria and his wife Henriette Adelaide of Savoy after…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nysa"><span class="enc-name">Nysa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nysa (German: Neisse) is a town in the Opole Voivodeship of southern Poland, on the Nysa Kłodzka river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyungu-ya-mawe"><span class="enc-name">Nyungu ya Mawe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nyungu ya Mawe was a Nyamwezi warlord of the 19th century in what is now western Tanzania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nzakara"><span class="enc-name">Nzakara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nzakara are a people of the south-east of the Central African Republic, living along the Mbomou River around Bangassou.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nzinga-a-nkuwu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nzinga-a-nkuwu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nzinga a Nkuwu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nzinga a Nkuwu was a king, or manikongo, of the Kingdom of Kongo in the late fifteenth century.</span></a></li></ul>
